  {
    "title": "Two servers, one token",
    "body": "Suppose each server **reads** a key's tokens, decides, and then **writes** the new value. Two servers read \"1 token left\" at the same moment, both allow their request, and both write 0: **two** requests got through on one token, and nothing reports an error. The fix is one **atomic** step in the store: a small script that refills by the time passed, takes a token if there is one, and saves the result. No other request can run between those parts, so only one of the two gets the last token.",
